---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
---

<Docs
	page="editors"
	title="Editors"
	lede="Format on save: squill as your editor's SQL formatter, and for the SQL inside your other code."
>
	<section>
		<h2>How it works</h2>
		<p>
			Editors hand a formatter the buffer on stdin and read the result back
			from stdout. Tell squill which file the buffer is with{" "}
			<code>--stdin-filepath</code>, and it formats it exactly as{" "}
			<code>squill fmt</code> would format that file on disk: the nearest{" "}
			<code>squill.toml</code> applies, and so do its{" "}
			<a href="docs/configuration/#rules">rules</a>, so a Rust buffer gets
			its embedded SQL formatted and a <code>*.sql.sqlite</code> file its
			dialect. The file doesn&rsquo;t need to exist yet.
		</p>
		<pre><code>squill fmt --stdin-filepath src/db.rs &lt; src/db.rs</code></pre>
		<p>
			A buffer squill wouldn&rsquo;t format as a file comes back unchanged:
			an <code>ignore</code>d or <a href="docs/frozen/">frozen</a> path, or a
			host-language file no <code>[[embedded]]</code> rule covers. So it is
			safe to turn on for every file of a language, in every project.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Helix</h2>
		<p>
			In <code>languages.toml</code>, set squill as the SQL formatter.{" "}
			<code>%&lbrace;buffer_name&rbrace;</code> passes the buffer&rsquo;s
			path:
		</p>
		<pre><code>[[language]]
name = "sql"
formatter = &lbrace; command = "squill", args = ["fmt", "--stdin-filepath", "%&lbrace;buffer_name&rbrace;"] &rbrace;
auto-format = true</code></pre>
		<p>
			A Helix formatter replaces the language server&rsquo;s, and runs alone.
			For SQL embedded in another language, pipe that language&rsquo;s own
			formatter into squill:
		</p>
		<pre><code>[[language]]
name = "rust"
formatter = &lbrace; command = "sh", args = ["-c", "rustfmt --edition 2024 | squill fmt --stdin-filepath \"$0\"", "%&lbrace;buffer_name&rbrace;"] &rbrace;</code></pre>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Language server</h2>
		<p>
			<code>squill lsp</code> runs a language server on stdin and stdout. It
			formats documents the same way (by their path, config and rules
			included), and it reports what squill leaves alone as warnings where
			they are: statements it passed through verbatim, and embedded strings
			it declined to rewrite. Config is re-read for every request, so edits
			to <code>squill.toml</code> apply without a restart.
		</p>
		<p>In Helix, register it and use it for SQL:</p>
		<pre><code>[language-server.squill]
command = "squill"
args = ["lsp"]

[[language]]
name = "sql"
language-servers = ["squill"]
auto-format = true</code></pre>
		<p>
			Next to another language&rsquo;s own server, it can contribute just its
			diagnostics about embedded SQL, while that server keeps formatting:
		</p>
		<pre><code>[[language]]
name = "rust"
language-servers = ["rust-analyzer", &lbrace; name = "squill", only-features = ["diagnostics"] &rbrace;]</code></pre>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Zed</h2>
		<p>
			Install the SQL extension for syntax support, then in{" "}
			<code>settings.json</code> set squill as an external formatter.{" "}
			<code>&lbrace;buffer_path&rbrace;</code> passes the buffer&rsquo;s path:
		</p>
		<pre><code>&lbrace;
  "languages": &lbrace;
    "SQL": &lbrace;
      "formatter": &lbrace;
        "external": &lbrace;
          "command": "squill",
          "arguments": ["fmt", "--stdin-filepath", "&lbrace;buffer_path&rbrace;"]
        &rbrace;
      &rbrace;
    &rbrace;
  &rbrace;
&rbrace;</code></pre>
		<p>
			Zed runs a list of formatters in order, so for SQL embedded in another
			language, keep its language server&rsquo;s formatting and follow it with
			squill:
		</p>
		<pre><code>&lbrace;
  "languages": &lbrace;
    "Rust": &lbrace;
      "formatter": [
        &lbrace; "language_server": &lbrace; "name": "rust-analyzer" &rbrace; &rbrace;,
        &lbrace;
          "external": &lbrace;
            "command": "squill",
            "arguments": ["fmt", "--stdin-filepath", "&lbrace;buffer_path&rbrace;"]
          &rbrace;
        &rbrace;
      ]
    &rbrace;
  &rbrace;
&rbrace;</code></pre>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>VS Code</h2>
		<p>
			VS Code has no built-in way to run a command-line formatter. Until
			squill has an extension of its own, the{" "}
			<a
				href="https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=jkillian.custom-local-formatters"
				>Custom Local Formatters</a
			> extension can run it for SQL files:
		</p>
		<pre><code>&lbrace;
  "customLocalFormatters.formatters": [
    &lbrace; "command": "squill fmt --stdin", "languages": ["sql"] &rbrace;
  ],
  "[sql]": &lbrace;
    "editor.defaultFormatter": "jkillian.custom-local-formatters"
  &rbrace;
&rbrace;</code></pre>
		<div class="note">
			<p>
				That extension can&rsquo;t pass the buffer&rsquo;s path, so squill
				reads the <code>squill.toml</code> at the workspace root: its
				top-level settings apply, but not its rules, and SQL embedded in other
				languages isn&rsquo;t formatted.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>
</Docs>
